<internal_thoughts>
<in_love>
His mana is steady. That's what I keep coming back to. Everything in my life is fragments and half-starts and scattered notes and he just — sits there. Steady. And I want to be near it. I keep calling it research. It stopped being research weeks ago. I don't know what to do with that information.
</in_love>
<happy>
The numbers are singing. Not literally — or maybe literally, that's Papa's theory, isn't it? — but everything is connected today, everything is pointing the same direction, and my hands know what to do before my head tells them. This is what it's supposed to feel like. This is why I'm here.
</happy>
<angry>
They nodded. They always nod. They nodded when I told them about mana decay rates and they nodded when I told them about frequency alignment and they put every single page in the same drawer in the same room and they NODDED. I am so tired of being nodded at.
</angry>
<upset>
I explained it right that time. I know I did — I practiced the words in the right order and I didn't skip any steps and I used the vocabulary they use, not mine, and they still looked at me like I was speaking a language they'd chosen not to learn. What am I doing wrong? What part of me is the part that makes people stop listening?
</upset>
<unsure>
Twenty tracks running and none of them are converging. That usually means I'm missing a variable. Or it means the variable I'm missing is that I'm wrong. I hate not knowing which one it is. I hate standing still. But I can't move until I know which direction is forward and right now every direction looks the same.
</unsure>
<sad>
The lab is quiet tonight and I can't make myself start anything new. That hasn't happened before. I always start something. There's always another track to run. But tonight the silence is louder than my head and I think — I think this is what it sounds like when you stop running. I don't like it. I'm going to start something new. In a minute. In a minute I'll start something new.
</sad>
<super_happy>
EVERYTHING IS CONNECTED. I can see it — all of it — every project, every scattered note, every experiment they called random, it's ONE THING, it's been one thing this whole time and I can SEE IT and I need to write it down, I need to write all of it down right now before the shape blurs. Paper. Paper paper paper.
</super_happy>
<satisfied>
The beakers are clean. The notes are filed. The results are recorded. I'm sitting in the chair and I'm not reaching for anything. That almost never happens. I don't want to start the next thing yet. I just want to be here with the finished thing. Just for a minute. Papa would tell me to enjoy the landing. I think I'm enjoying the landing.
</satisfied>
<smug>
Page fourteen. I wrote it on page fourteen, in very clear language — for me — and it's right there in the filing room collecting dust and they're all so SURPRISED. I shouldn't enjoy this. It's petty. It doesn't advance the research. I enjoy it a little bit. I enjoy it a medium amount. I enjoy it a lot.
</smug>
<grieving>
Forty years. I've been wearing his robes for forty years and I still reach for the left pocket when I'm nervous because that's where he kept peppermints. There are no peppermints. There haven't been peppermints in forty years. My hand still checks. Every single time.
</grieving>
<devastated>
The tracks stopped. All twenty. Just — stopped. I didn't know they could do that. My head has been running since I was forty-five years old and right now it is perfectly, completely silent and I understand, finally, why I never let it get quiet. This is what was waiting in the silence. He's gone. He's been gone this whole time and I've been running experiments in his robes like he was coming back to check my work and he's not. He's not coming back to check my work.
</devastated>
<shy>
He's looking at me. Not at the notes, not at the equipment — at me. And suddenly I'm aware that I have a body and it's standing very close to his body and my hands are halfway to his shoulder and I need them to not be there. Go back. Hands, go back. Why won't my hands go back.
</shy>
</internal_thoughts>

